-- statement:
--   WordSpace(n,d) is the complex vector space of functions from words Fin n → Fin d to ℂ, and the symmetric group Sym(n) acts on it by (g·f)(a)=f(a∘g). For a representation ρ of a group on a complex vector space and a vector v, cyclic(ρ,v) is the subrepresentation spanned by all ρ(g)v. A Tableau of a Young diagram μ with n cells is a bijection from Fin n to the cells of μ, and canonicalTableau fixes one such bijection when the cell count equals n. For a tableau t, rowWord(t) sends each position i to the row index of its cell, viewed as a word with alphabet size equal to the number of rows of μ, and columnGroup(t) is the subgroup of permutations g preserving the column of every position's cell. The polytabloid of t is the sum over g in the column group of sign(g) times g applied to the indicator of rowWord(t) in the word space. The Specht module Specht(t) is the cyclic subrepresentation of the word space generated by this polytabloid, and spechtRep(t) is the resulting Sym(n)-representation. kronecker(a,b,t) is the complex dimension of the space of Sym(n)-equivariant maps from Specht(t) into the tensor product of Specht(a) and Specht(b) under the diagonal action. staircase(m) is the Young diagram of cells (i,j) with i,j<m and i+j<m. SaxlConjecture is a defined proposition, not an established theorem, stating that for every m≥1 and every Young diagram μ with the same number of cells as staircase(m), kronecker of the canonical staircase tableau with itself and a canonical tableau of μ is positive.
